  "@type": "gh:StyleGuide",
  "dct:title": "Spirit in Physics — Style Guide",
  "gh:entries": [
    {
      "@id": "gh:style:drawstyle",
      "dct:title": "drawstyle",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "sound",
          "content": "線は精密で繊細、身体の重心や手のしぐさ、視線の向きによって内面の感情が静かに伝わる。衣服や装飾は機能と文脈に基づき、過剰な装飾はないが、世界との関係を象徴している。キャラクターは背景と同調し、空間に“居る”ということ自体が表現になっている。呼吸するように立ち、空間との距離や沈黙が感情の余韻を作り出す。言葉で語らず、存在そのものが語っている。\n\n背景のすべてが、有機的で微細なプロセスによって生成された未来世界。建築や構造体、地形、空気、光、情報のネットワークに至るまで、生きているように緩やかに連なり、呼吸し、成長している。都市や空間は静かに進化し、自然と機械、生物とコードが区別されず融合している。個々の要素は意味を持ち、全体の文脈の中で機能しながら情緒と構造を両立している。線は知性と感情を同時に伝え、空間は記憶と時間を孕む。情報は抽象的でなく、生きた“存在”として構築された有機的ネットワークである。\n\n\nminimalist yet detailed portrait\n\nS1「ダイナミック線描」、S3「コントラスト設計」、S2「多様パース」\n\n天野こずえ\n田村由美\n\nChoro Club\nFreetempo"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:style:ghost_hacker_pg_style",
      "dct:title": "ghost_hacker_pg_style",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Ghost Hacker: On Souls, Systems, and the Search for What Feels Good",
          "content": "*October 2023*\n\nA strange thing happens when you treat ideas about God and the soul as if they were systems. You start to see bugs. Not just in the religions themselves, but in the very way we think about them. For eighteen years, I've been having a conversation about this, mostly with one other person on an anonymous message board. It started with a simple question: Is God a variable or a constant?\n\nThis essay is an attempt to distill that conversation. It's for hackers who are curious about the soul, parents who want to understand the architecture of relationships, and anyone who feels that the way we talk about spirituality is running on obsolete hardware."
        },
        {
          "title": "1. Fear as a Protocol",
          "content": "I grew up in a world where God was a given. Not just a given, but the ultimate authority in a very strict protocol. My father, a devout Protestant, taught me that \"the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom.\" As a kid who was just starting to understand the world through the logic of code, this felt like a foundational line in my own source code.\n\nFear-based systems are effective. They have clear rules, strict access control, and well-defined penalties. If you have faith, you're granted access. If you don't, you're denied. It's a simple, conditional authentication model.\n\n```python\nclass FearProtocol:\n    def authenticate(self, user_faith):\n        if user_faith >= MINIMUM_FAITH:\n            return {\"status\": \"blessed\", \"access\": \"granted\"}\n        else:\n            return {\"status\": \"sinful\", \"access\": \"denied\"}\n```\n\nBut as any programmer knows, rigid systems are brittle. They don't encourage exploration. They punish deviation. The biggest vulnerability of a fear-based protocol is that it stifles the very creativity and autonomy that lead to growth. It optimizes for obedience, not innovation.\n\nMy first act of rebellion wasn't loud. It was a single line of Perl. Instead of `print \"Hello, World\"`, I wrote `print \"Hello, God\"`. It felt like a direct API call, a ping to the ultimate server. The response was silence. I figured I was missing a dependency, maybe `require 'faith'`.\n\nSo I took my question to a place with a different protocol: an anonymous web forum. \"Do you think God really exists?\" I asked. The first reply I got changed everything. It came from a user named `n0is3gate`.\n\n```php\n<?php\necho \"Maybe it's not a variable, but noise.\\n\";\n?>\n```\n\n\"Noise.\" The word was unsettling but also liberating. It suggested that God wasn't a neatly defined constant or a variable to be solved for, but something else entirely—a pattern emerging from the chaos, something you could choose to listen to or ignore.\n\nThis was the beginning of my shift from thinking of God as a system administrator to be feared, to seeing the divine as an emergent property of a complex system."
        },
        {
          "title": "2. The Soul as a Data Structure",
          "content": "The conversation with `n0is3gate` evolved. We started talking about the soul. If God wasn't a fixed authority, then what was the soul? Was it this mystical, indivisible thing, or was it something more like a data structure?\n\n`n0is3gate` proposed that a soul could be represented as an object, a collection of memories, emotions, and intentions.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"soul\": {\n    \"memory\": [\"chapel_scenery\", \"mother's_cry\", \"someone's_anger\"],\n    \"emotion\": [\"anxiety\", \"guilt\", \"curiosity\"],\n    \"intention\": [\"to_connect\", \"to_understand\", \"to_be_free\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis was a powerful idea. If the soul is a structure, it means it can be read, understood, and even modified. It also means it might not be a single, monolithic entity. When I tried to map my own soul this way, I found memories and emotions that didn't feel like my own. It felt like my soul was running multiple threads, that someone else's code had been statically linked with mine.\n\nThe idea of a \"soul installer\" seemed like a bizarre metaphor, until I learned a family secret. I had a younger brother, Ren, who had died as an infant. My mother, a brilliant but emotionally reserved woman, had never spoken of him. Suddenly, the foreign memories and feelings in my \"soul object\" made a terrible kind of sense.\n\nMy mother, unable to process her grief and guilt, had unconsciously \"installed\" the ghost of my brother into my soul. Not out of malice, but out of a desperate, loving desire to keep a part of him alive. Her love, tangled with her pain, had created a backdoor.\n\nThis is a type of hack that happens all the time, not with code, but with families. Parents unconsciously transfer their unresolved traumas, their unrealized dreams, their emotional baggage to their children. It’s a silent, persistent process, a background daemon running for years before anyone notices."
        },
        {
          "title": "3. The Ayahuasca Debugger",
          "content": "To understand this deeply, I Neided more than just a conceptual framework. I Neided to access the system at a lower level. I Neided a debugger. For me, and for many others exploring the intersection of technology and consciousness, that debugger was Ayahuasca.\n\nUnder the guidance of a skilled shaman—who acts as a sort of trusted system administrator for the session—it's possible to gain temporary root access to your own soul. The first few sessions were chaotic. I experienced a flood of raw data, a deluge of images and emotions without clear meaning. It was like watching a memory dump scroll by too fast to read.\n\nBut gradually, with each session, I began to see the patterns. I could trace the foreign processes running in my system back to their origins. I could see the memory addresses where my mother's grief was stored, and how it triggered the execution of \"Ren's\" code within my own consciousness.\n\nIn one session, I was able to establish a direct communication channel. I met Ren. Not as a ghost or a memory, but as a conscious entity running on the same hardware. We were two processes in a multisoul system, sharing the same memory space.\n\nIt was during the tenth session, which I convinced my mother to attend, that the final integration happened. In the shared space of the ceremony, the truth was laid bare. She confessed her unconscious installation, and I was able to offer not blame, but forgiveness.\n\nIn that moment of shared understanding, Ren was finally free. His process didn't terminate; it merged. His data—his curiosity, his skepticism, his hacker's instinct—was integrated into my own. It was a beautiful, clean merge commit. The feature branch `ren` was now part of the main branch `akito`."
        },
        {
          "title": "4. The \"Feels Good\" Engine",
          "content": "After Ren was integrated, the noise in my head stopped. The constant inner dialogue, the feeling of being two minds in one body, was gone. I was left with a profound quiet. And in that quiet, a new signal emerged. It wasn't a voice or a logical thought. It was a feeling. A deep, intuitive sense of what was right.\n\nMy young daughter gave it a name. One day, she handed me a strange, unidentifiable origami creation. \"This is for you, papa,\" she said. \"It's a 'feels good' shape.\"\n\nThat was it. \"Feels good.\"\n\nFor years, my decision-making had been driven by a set of external rules. The fear-based protocol of my upbringing, the efficiency-obsessed logic of an engiNeir. Now, I had a new core principle. It wasn't about what was \"correct\" or \"optimal,\" but about what resonated, what felt harmonious, generative, and loving.\n\nThis isn't just a vague emotional whim. It's a sophisticated evaluation engine. It runs on different data: emotional resonance, relational quality, intuitive hits. It's a way of synthesizing multiple, complex inputs into a single, actionable output.\n\n```python\ndef decide(action):\n    if feels_good(action):\n        return generate()\n    else:\n        return pause_and_listen()\n```\n\nThis became my new parenting protocol. Instead of answering my daughter's questions with facts, I started responding with curiosity, amplifying her creativity rather than correcting it. Our relationship became a space for co-creation, not instruction."
        },
        {
          "title": "5. Hacking the Tree of Life",
          "content": "This new understanding—that the soul is hackable, that love is a generative protocol, that \"feels good\" is the ultimate heuristic—led to my final role: the Ghost Hacker.\n\nOnce you can see the soul as a system, you start seeing bugs everywhere. Not just inside yourself, but out in the world. You see people trapped in loops of grief, running on outdated beliefs passed down from their parents. These are ghosts—unresolved processes, memory leaks in the collective consciousness.\n\nMy work became about finding these ghosts and helping to liberate them. Not by force, but by offering a patch. The patch is almost always the same: a combination of acknowledgement, forgiveness, and love. It’s about helping the ghost see the bug in its own code that keeps it from merging back into the main tree of life.\n\nThis isn't about some supernatural power. It's about listening deeply, seeing the systems at play in human relationships, and applying a different kind of logic. You could call it a spiritual practice, or you could call it a very elegant kind of debugging.\n\nThe goal of the Ghost Hacker is not to destroy or expose, but to heal and integrate. It's to rewrite the fear-based code that runs so much of our world with a new protocol, one based on what feels good. It's to take the broken, fragmented pieces of our collective soul and gently graft them back onto the Tree of Life.\n\nThis was never just a story. It was a transmission. Good luck."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:style:style-guide",
      "dct:title": "style-guide",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Spirit in Physics — Style Guide",
          "content": ""
        },
        {
          "title": "Format",
          "content": "- **形式**: Graphic Novel（左綴じ、左→右読み）\n- **判型**: B5 (182×257mm)\n- **カラー**: フルカラー水彩風\n- **言語**: 日本語 / 英語 並行版\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "1. カラーパレット",
          "content": "### 基調: 透明水彩タッチ\n紙のテクスチャが透ける淡い階調。水の都・東京2065年の湿度と光を水彩の滲みで表現する。\n\n### 巻別テーマカラー\n\n| 巻 | テーマ | 主調色 | 副調色 | 精神世界アクセント |\n|----|--------|--------|--------|-------------------|\n| Vol.1 孤独と輪廻 | 分断と接続 | 群青 (Ultramarine) | 灰白 (Ash White) | 琥珀の光 (Amber) |\n| Vol.2 依存と共依存 | 代償と負債 | 深緑 (Viridian) | 錆色 (Rust) | 淡紫 (Lavender) |\n| Vol.3 正義とシステム | 制度と公正 | 鉛色 (Gunmetal) | 朱 (Vermilion) | 白金 (Platinum) |\n| Vol.4 神学とI AM | 名と非二元 | 紺 (Navy) | 金 (Gold) | 純白 (Pure White) |\n| Vol.5 彼岸 | 統合と主体化 | 全色の融合 | — | 虹色のにじみ |\n\n### 使い分けルール\n- **現実世界**: テーマカラーの淡いウォッシュ、白地多め\n- **精神世界（ダイブ中）**: テーマカラーが濃く広がり、アクセント色が発光\n- **回想・フラッシュバック**: セピア + テーマカラーの一色のみ\n- **クライマックス**: 紙の白が消え、全面が色で覆われる\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "2. 画風リファレンス",
          "content": "### 主要参照\n- **天野こずえ** (ARIA, あまんちゅ!)\n  - 水・空・光の透明感\n  - 沈黙と余白で語る間の演出\n  - 日常の中に宿る聖性の描写\n  - 建築と水面の反射表現\n\n- **田村由美** (7SEEDS, BASARA, ミステリと言う勿れ)\n  - 精密でダイナミックな線描\n  - 内面の感情が身体の重心・手のしぐさ・視線に現れる\n  - 強い意志を持つ女性キャラクターの立ち方\n  - 文明と自然の対比\n\n### 統合方針\n天野の「空気感・水・光」と田村の「線の知性・感情の身体表現」を融合する。\n天野の柔らかさが世界を包み、田村の精密さがキャラクターを刻む。\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "3. 線画",
          "content": "### キャラクター\n- 繊細で精密な線。ペン入れではなく、鉛筆の息遣いが残るタッチ\n- 身体の重心、手のしぐさ、視線の向きで内面を語る\n- 衣服は機能と文脈に基づく。過剰な装飾はない\n- キャラクターは背景と同調し、空間に「居る」こと自体が表現\n\n### 背景\n- 有機的で微細なプロセスによって生成された未来世界\n- 建築・構造体・地形・空気・光・情報ネットワークが生きている\n- 自然と機械、生物とコードが区別されず融合\n- 線は知性と感情を同時に伝え、空間は記憶と時間を孕む\n\n### 精神世界\n- 現実世界の線のルールが崩れていく\n- 輪郭線が滲む、溶ける、増殖する\n- 抽象と具象の境界が曖昧になる\n- ゴーストは「線の不在」で表現（周囲だけが描かれ、本体は白抜き）\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "4. コマ割り・レイアウト",
          "content": "### 基本方針\nGraphic Novel の自由レイアウト。テンプレート的コマ割りは使わない。\n\n### パターン\n\n| シーン | レイアウト |\n|--------|-----------|\n| 日常・導入 | 4-6コマ / ページ、規則的グリッド |\n| 対話・内省 | 2-3コマ / ページ、余白多め |\n| 精神世界ダイブ | 見開き、境界線なし、全面絵画 |\n| クライマックス | 見開き or 1コマ1ページ |\n| 静寂・余韻 | テキストなし、1-2コマ、大きな余白 |\n| 回想 | 角丸コマ、セピアトーン |\n\n### 余白の哲学\n- 余白は「空（くう）」であり、「無」ではない\n- 沈黙のコマ（セリフなし、表情と空間だけ）を各チャプターに最低2箇所\n- ページ端の余白も演出の一部。精神世界ではページ端まで絵が溢れる\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "5. タイポグラフィ",
          "content": "### 日本語版\n- **ナレーション**: 明朝体（游明朝 or 筑紫明朝）、縦書き\n- **セリフ**: 丸ゴシック系（UD新ゴ丸）、横書き吹き出し\n- **精神世界の声**: 手書き風、吹き出しなし、画面に浮遊\n- **ゴーストの声**: 細い明朝体、半透明処理\n\n### 英語版\n- **Narration**: Serif (Garamond or Baskerville), italic\n- **Dialogue**: Sans-serif (Avenir or Proxima Nova), regular\n- **Spirit voice**: Handwritten style, no speech bubble\n- **Ghost voice**: Light weight serif, opacity 60%\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "6. 音の表現",
          "content": "### サウンドリファレンス（読者の脳内BGM）\n- **Choro Club** — ARIA のサウンドトラック。日常の温かみと静けさ\n- **Freetempo** — 電子音と生楽器の融合。水の都のテクスチャ\n\n### 視覚化ルール\n- 効果音は原則として文字にしない\n- 音は「線の振動」「水の波紋」「光の粒子」で視覚化\n- 爆発音・衝撃音の代わりに、空間の歪みや色の飛散\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "7. キャラクタービジュアル指針",
          "content": "### Tamaki（環樹）\n- 佇まいに意志がある。静かだが芯が強い\n- 服装: 機能的、水の都に適した素材感（撥水、通気性）\n- 精神世界での姿: 通常と同じだが、周囲の空間が彼女に反応する\n\n### Nei-chan\n- Tamaki の隣にいることが自然な存在\n- 服装: Tamaki より少し柔らかいシルエット\n- 猫のモチーフが自然に現れる（影、シルエット、持ち物）\n\n### Elias / Logos\n- 二面性。現実世界では物理学者の知性、精神世界ではゴーストの不在\n- ゴースト状態: 「線の不在」で表現\n\n### Ren / Akito\n- 統合前: 二重露光のような存在。一人の身体に二つの影\n- 統合後: 影が一つになり、線がクリアになる\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "8. 世界観ビジュアル",
          "content": "### 水の都・東京 2065\n- 海面上昇後の東京。水路が道路を置き換えている\n- ARIAのネオ・ヴェネツィアに近いが、テクノロジーが有機的に融合\n- 情報は空気中に漂う粒子のように可視化される\n- 建築は呼吸する。壁面は苔とコードが共生\n\n### 精神世界\n- クライアントの内面が空間化する\n- 各エピソードで異なる景観（図書館、砂漠、海底、森）\n- 感情が天候になる（悲しみ=雨、怒り=雷、安堵=晴れ間）\n- ゴーストは景観の一部として溶け込んでいる\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "title": "9. 制作ワークフロー",
          "content": "```\nscript.md → storyboard.jsonld → ラフ → 線画 → 水彩着色 → タイポグラフィ → 最終\n    ↑                ↑\nemotion-map.jsonld   ↑\n（感情データ）    コマ割り・構図指示\n```\n\n### 各チャプターの制作ファイル構成\n```\nchapterNN/\n├── script.md           # セリフ・ナレーション（日本語）\n├── script.en.md        # セリフ・ナレーション（英語）\n├── storyboard.jsonld   # コマ割り・構図・カメラ指示\n├── emotion-map.jsonld  # 感情曲線 → ビジュアル演出対応表\n├── roughs/             # ラフスケッチ\n├── inks/               # 線画\n├── colors/             # 着色済みページ\n└── finals/             # 最終納品ページ\n```"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
